<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    THE HERO OF SOVNGARDE HIMSELF.

    Mirrors Dovahkiin_AncientDragonbornKind, because he is built to that summon's pattern - the
    one part of this feature that is now playtested. He is generated INVISIBLE (his hediff
    carries vanilla's HediffComp_Invisibility), so the only thing on screen is his spectral
    armour and his greatsword, which is why nothing is generated onto him: apparel would never
    be seen, and every generated item is another object to clean up when he vanishes.

    combatPower 160 against the Ancient Dragonborn's 140. He is armoured more lightly - 0.66
    sharp / 0.33 blunt against 0.75/0.75 - but stays twice as long, and this number is what
    storytellers read when weighing the map, so it should reflect the whole package rather than
    just his plate.
  -->
  <PawnKindDef>
    <defName>Dovahkiin_CallOfValorKind</defName>
    <label>hero of sovngarde</label>
    <race>Human</race>
    <combatPower>160</combatPower>
    <isFighter>true</isFighter>
    <canBeSapper>false</canBeSapper>
    <acceptArrestChanceFactor>0</acceptArrestChanceFactor>
    <!-- Any category would do; he is invisible and his backstory is never read. Tribal is
         chosen only because it exists in Core and carries no tech assumptions. -->
    <backstoryFilters>
      <li>
        <categories>
          <li>Tribal</li>
        </categories>
      </li>
    </backstoryFilters>
    <!-- Nothing generated onto him. The greatsword is equipped in code so it is always the
         right weapon, and apparel would be invisible anyway. -->
    <apparelMoney>0~0</apparelMoney>
    <weaponMoney>0~0</weaponMoney>
    <techHediffsChance>0</techHediffsChance>
    <chemicalAddictionChance>0</chemicalAddictionChance>
    <apparelAllowHeadgearChance>0</apparelAllowHeadgearChance>
    <initialWillRange>0~0</initialWillRange>
    <initialResistanceRange>0~0</initialResistanceRange>
  </PawnKindDef>

  <!--
    CALL OF VALOR'S GREATSWORD.

    THE USER'S INSTRUCTION, VERBATIM: "medieval mod should recognise it as one it's weapon
    (using their same position, orientation when held by a pawn, and same animation too since
    melee animation is in the modlist, also the weapon it should behave like is the greatsword
    in the blades section)."

    So this is the Ancient Dragonborn halberd treatment applied to a blade: OUR art, THEIR
    behaviour. Everything below that looks like a magic number was read out of
    DankPyon_MeleeWeapon_Greatsword and its parent DankPyon_Base_SharpSword_Oversize on disk
    2026-08-01, not remembered and not copied from a document.

    THE PARENT IS A DIFFERENT SHAPE FROM THE HALBERD'S, AND THAT MATTERS.
    The halberd inherits DankPyon_Base_Sharp_Oversize, which carries a LIVE
    <equippedAngleOffset>45</equippedAngleOffset> - so our axe def sets 45 and is done.
    DankPyon_Base_SharpSword_Oversize does NOT: its equippedAngleOffset is commented out, and
    the hold pose comes from a VFECore ThingDefExtension instead, with a separate AUTHORED
    angle and offset PER FACING. Copying the axe's approach here would have produced a
    weapon held at one fixed angle, which is not how their greatsword is held.

    NO weaponTags, DELIBERATELY. Their greatsword carries <li>DankPyon_Greatsword</li> and
    <li>MedievalMeleeAdvanced</li>, and those are how PawnKindDefs pick weapons to spawn with.
    Inheriting them would let medieval raiders and traders turn up carrying a spectral summon's
    sword. "Recognise it as one of its weapons" means the hold, the swing and the numbers - the
    user listed exactly those in their own brackets - not entry into the loot tables.
  -->
  <ThingDef>
    <defName>Dovahkiin_ValorGreatsword</defName>
    <label>spectral greatsword</label>
    <description>A greatsword of solid Thu'um, carried by a hero of Sovngarde answering the Dragonborn's call. It is not steel and it is not here for long.</description>
    <thingClass>ThingWithComps</thingClass>
    <category>Item</category>
    <equipmentType>Primary</equipmentType>
    <altitudeLayer>Item</altitudeLayer>
    <drawerType>MapMeshOnly</drawerType>
    <tickerType>Never</tickerType>
    <useHitPoints>false</useHitPoints>
    <selectable>true</selectable>
    <techLevel>Medieval</techLevel>
    <smeltable>false</smeltable>
    <burnableByRecipe>false</burnableByRecipe>
    <destroyOnDrop>true</destroyOnDrop>

    <!-- REQUIRED, and its absence is what killed the Ancient Dragonborn's first summon outright.
         Without it RimWorld logs "is equipment but has no CompEquippable" as a CONFIG ERROR at
         load - which a clean build and an XML parse check both pass - and then
         Pawn_EquipmentTracker.Notify_EquipmentAdded throws the moment anything holds it.
         There is NO CompProperties_Equippable type: vanilla's BaseWeapon declares it as a plain
         comp carrying compClass. Writing a weapon standalone to escape stuff and quality drops
         this comp, because it lives on that base. -->
    <comps>
      <li>
        <compClass>CompEquippable</compClass>
      </li>
    </comps>

    <!-- destroyOnDrop with the default tradeability of All is a config error in its own right,
         and None is correct anyway: a spectral weapon that exists for a few hours has no
         business in a trade window. -->
    <tradeability>None</tradeability>

    <!-- Standard_Pickup. There is no Interact_Bladelike* def in Core and an unrecognised sound
         defName is an XML error at load. Checked on disk, not assumed. -->
    <soundInteract>Standard_Pickup</soundInteract>

    <graphicData>
      <texPath>Things/Item/Equipment/ValorGreatsword</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Single</graphicClass>
      <!-- (1.25,1.25) - DankPyon_MeleeWeapon_Greatsword's own drawSize. NOT the halberd's 1.5.
           WeaponTweakData passes ScaleX/ScaleY 1.25 to match; those two must agree or Melee
           Animation draws the weapon at a different size from the one the map shows. -->
      <drawSize>(1.25,1.25)</drawSize>
    </graphicData>
    <uiIconScale>1.2</uiIconScale>

    <!-- NO <equippedAngleOffset>. Read that as a decision, not an omission.
         Their greatsword has none either - DankPyon_Base_SharpSword_Oversize has it commented
         out - because the VFECore extension below supplies a different angle for every facing.
         Adding one here would fight those per-facing angles rather than complement them. -->

    <modExtensions>
      <!-- THE HOLD POSE, copied verbatim from DankPyon_Base_SharpSword_Oversize.
           These are AUTHORED PER-FACING POSES, not one angle plus a rotation - north is 115
           while south and east are -45 - which is exactly why the preview sheet stuck the sword
           sideways out of his back until north got its own number.

           MayRequire, because CLAUDE.md invariant 5 says the mod must load clean on the
           baseline environment (Core + Royalty + Ideology + Harmony + HugsLib) and VFECore is
           not in it. An unguarded li naming a class that cannot resolve logs an XML error and
           is dropped - the def still loads, so this would be a silent red line in the log
           rather than a crash, which is worse. VEF is active in this modlist
           (oskarpotocki.vanillafactionsexpanded.core, verified in ModsConfig.xml).

           On the baseline the sword simply draws at the default angle. Medieval Overhaul is not
           present there either, so nothing it would have matched is present to disagree with. -->
      <li Class="VFECore.ThingDefExtension" MayRequire="oskarpotocki.vanillafactionsexpanded.core">
        <weaponDraftedDrawOffsets>
          <north>
            <drawOffset>(-0.20,0.0,0.20)</drawOffset>
            <angleOffset>115</angleOffset>
          </north>
          <east>
            <drawOffset>(0.30,0.00,0.20)</drawOffset>
            <angleOffset>-45</angleOffset>
          </east>
          <south>
            <drawOffset>(0.30,0.00,0.20)</drawOffset>
            <angleOffset>-45</angleOffset>
          </south>
          <west>
            <drawOffset>(-0.30,0.0,0.20)</drawOffset>
            <angleOffset>45</angleOffset>
          </west>
        </weaponDraftedDrawOffsets>
      </li>
    </modExtensions>

    <statBases>
      <!-- Mass 3, theirs. The halberd is 2.75; these are different weapons. -->
      <Mass>3</Mass>
      <MarketValue>0</MarketValue>
    </statBases>

    <weaponClasses>
      <li>Melee</li>
    </weaponClasses>

    <!--
      DankPyon_MeleeWeapon_Greatsword's tools, verbatim. A player with Medieval Overhaul gets a
      weapon that hits exactly like the greatsword they know; a player without it still gets a
      properly weighted two-hander rather than numbers invented on the spot. No MayRequire and
      no fallback is needed, because we borrow the BEHAVIOUR and ship our own art - which is
      also the licensing line: their art is a ruler, never a source.
    -->
    <tools>
      <li>
        <label>handle</label>
        <capacities>
          <li>Poke</li>
        </capacities>
        <power>9</power>
        <cooldownTime>2</cooldownTime>
      </li>
      <li>
        <label>edge</label>
        <labelUsedInLogging>false</labelUsedInLogging>
        <capacities>
          <li>Cut</li>
        </capacities>
        <power>31.25</power>
        <cooldownTime>3.15</cooldownTime>
        <armorPenetration>0.25</armorPenetration>
      </li>
    </tools>
  </ThingDef>

</Defs>
